# Integrations — our own OAuth connector

A connection links this nexus to an outside platform **at the account level**, so our agents operate
those platforms directly. We looked hard at Nango (Elastic-licensed) and chose to **build the seam
ourselves** — the OAuth mechanics are public knowledge, and the credential-at-rest model is something
we already have (`Nexus.ControlPlane.Env`, AES-256-GCM, fail-closed). What we borrowed from Nango is
purely factual: the per-provider OAuth endpoints + scopes (their `providers.yaml` is a public catalog
of facts, not code). This connector is therefore generic mechanism — any deployer wires their own
providers through the catalog below, with **none of our keys baked in**.

## Two layers, never confused

- **Integration (this file)** = *auth*. Connect an account, hold its sealed tokens, refresh them.
  One account can back many capabilities (one Google connection → gmail + gcloud + drive toolkits).
- **Toolkit** = *capability*. The wasm-CLI an agent runs. A toolkit consumes a connection's token via
  `Cloud.Integrations.fresh_token/2` — it never sees a client secret and never does the OAuth dance.

So we start here (auth), and toolkits reference connections later. The dashboard's Integrations view
is the marketplace; the credential plumbing is all below.

## How a connection is made

**OAuth2 providers** (GitHub, Google, Linear, Asana, Jira) use the popup handoff so a secret never
touches the browser: the dashboard asks for an authorize URL, opens it in a popup, the provider
redirects to our `/cloud/oauth/callback`, we exchange `code → tokens` **server-side**, seal the
access + refresh tokens as two separate encrypted secrets, store the connection record, and the popup
closes. **API-key providers** (Cloudflare, Composio) skip OAuth — you paste a scoped token, we seal it.

Client app credentials (`<PROVIDER>_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID` / `_CLIENT_SECRET`) are read ONLY through
`Nexus.Secrets` — injected at deploy, our actual app keys are our DeployKit config, never in source.

server :integrations do
  alias Nexus.ControlPlane, as: CP
  alias Nexus.ControlPlane.Env

  # Pending OAuth handshakes are keyed by a single-use random `state`, parked under a reserved system
  # org so the callback (which has no tenant context of its own) can resolve the real tenant from it.
  @oauth_org "_oauth"

  route "GET /cloud/toolkits", :toolkits, auth: :user
  route "POST /cloud/toolkits/:id/toggle", :toggle_toolkit, auth: :member
  route "GET /cloud/integrations", :list_integrations, auth: :user
  route "POST /cloud/integrations", :connect_api_key, auth: :member
  route "POST /cloud/integrations/cli", :connect_cli, auth: :member
  route "GET /cloud/integrations/authorize", :authorize, auth: :user
  route "GET /cloud/oauth/callback", :callback, auth: :public
  route "GET /cloud/integrations/google/delegation", :google_delegation, auth: :user
  route "POST /cloud/integrations/google/delegation", :connect_google_dwd, auth: :member
  route "POST /cloud/integrations/google/:id/mailbox", :google_create_mailbox, auth: :member
  route "POST /cloud/integrations/google/:id/alias", :google_add_alias, auth: :member
  route "POST /cloud/integrations/google/:id/sendas", :google_add_sendas, auth: :member
  route "DELETE /cloud/integrations/:id", :remove_integration, auth: :member
  route "GET /cloud/toolkits/:id/detail", :toolkit_detail, auth: :user
  route "POST /cloud/integrations/:id/scopes", :set_scopes, auth: :member

  # ── the catalog — central config, the only opinion in this file ───────────────────────────────
  # OAuth endpoints + scopes are transcribed facts (public provider docs / Nango's providers.yaml).
  # `mode`: :oauth2 (popup handshake) | :api_key (paste a token). Generic — swap in your own.
  def catalog do
    [
      %{id: "github", name: "GitHub", group: "Source", mode: :oauth2,
        blurb: "The git nexus integration — repos, actions, packages, members through our app on the control plane.",
        authorize_url: "https://github.com/login/oauth/authorize",
        token_url: "https://github.com/login/oauth/access_token",
        scopes: "repo read:org workflow", scope_sep: " ", auth_params: %{}},
      # Google Workspace via the `gws` CLI (github.com/googleworkspace/cli) with BRING-YOUR-OWN auth.
      # No vendored client id, no service account of ours: the customer sets up their OWN Google Cloud
      # project (`gws auth setup`, or a Desktop OAuth client) and brings the resulting credential — a
      # short-lived access token, or a credentials/service-account JSON. We seal it and inject it into
      # the sandboxed `gws` run via env (`connection_env/2`). We are never the Google middleman.
      %{id: "google", name: "Google Workspace", group: "Workspace", mode: :cli, cli: "gws",
        blurb: "Drive, Gmail, Calendar, Admin via the gws CLI — on your own Google Cloud, no shared client id.",
        detail: "Runs the Google Workspace CLI (gws) in the sandbox against your own Google Cloud project. You set up auth once on your side (gws tells you how — it creates the project, enables APIs, logs you in); we only hold the sealed credential and inject it at run time.",
        setup_url: "https://github.com/googleworkspace/cli#authentication",
        cred_kinds: [
          %{id: "token", label: "Access token", hint: "gws auth export, or gcloud auth print-access-token", env: "GOOGLE_WORKSPACE_CLI_TOKEN"},
          %{id: "credentials_file", label: "Credentials JSON", hint: "client_secret.json / service-account.json contents", env: "GOOGLE_WORKSPACE_CLI_CREDENTIALS_FILE"}
        ]},
      %{id: "gmail", name: "Google (personal)", group: "Workspace", mode: :oauth2,
        blurb: "Connect your own Gmail & Drive — the agent acts as you, no admin needed.",
        authorize_url: "https://accounts.google.com/o/oauth2/v2/auth",
        token_url: "https://oauth2.googleapis.com/token",
        scopes: "openid email profile https://www.googleapis.com/auth/gmail.modify https://www.googleapis.com/auth/drive.file",
        scope_sep: " ", auth_params: %{"access_type" => "offline", "prompt" => "consent"}},
      %{id: "slack", name: "Slack", group: "Messaging", mode: :oauth2,
        blurb: "Workspace messaging — post, read channels, react, and respond as an agent.",
        authorize_url: "https://slack.com/oauth/v2/authorize",
        token_url: "https://slack.com/api/oauth.v2.access",
        scopes: "channels:read chat:write users:read", scope_sep: ",", auth_params: %{}},
      %{id: "fal", name: "fal.ai", group: "AI", mode: :api_key,
        blurb: "Image, video & audio generation on serverless GPUs.",
        field: "API key", hint: "key_id:key_secret"},
      %{id: "openrouter", name: "OpenRouter", group: "AI", mode: :api_key,
        blurb: "One API across 300+ LLMs — routing, fallbacks, and price/perf control.",
        field: "API key", hint: "sk-or-…"},
      # Roadmap integrations — shown as "Coming soon" until their capability is wired (soon: true).
      %{id: "asana", name: "Asana", group: "Project", mode: :oauth2, soon: true,
        blurb: "Mirror an Asana project into the nexus — query tasks and (gated) write back.",
        detail: "Federates Asana tasks into the nexus as first-class task nodes: agents query the project, plan against it, and write updates back behind an approval gate. One connection per Asana workspace.",
        authorize_url: "https://app.asana.com/-/oauth_authorize",
        token_url: "https://app.asana.com/-/oauth_token",
        scopes: "default", scope_sep: " ", auth_params: %{}},
      %{id: "linear", name: "Linear", group: "Project", mode: :oauth2, soon: true,
        blurb: "Mirror a Linear project into the nexus — query issues and (gated) write back.",
        detail: "Federates Linear issues into the nexus: agents read cycles + issues, plan against them, and post updates back behind an approval gate. Connect a Linear workspace.",
        authorize_url: "https://linear.app/oauth/authorize",
        token_url: "https://api.linear.app/oauth/token",
        scopes: "read,write", scope_sep: ",", auth_params: %{"prompt" => "consent"}},
      %{id: "cloudflare", name: "Cloudflare", group: "Infra", mode: :api_key, soon: true,
        blurb: "Deploy to Cloudflare — D1, Workers AI, Pages — on your own account (includes Wrangler).",
        detail: "An all-Cloudflare backend for a forged workbook: D1 database, Workers AI, and Pages hosting on the customer's own account. Bundles the Wrangler CLI for deploys. Connect with a scoped API token.",
        field: "API token", hint: "scoped account token"},
      %{id: "stripe", name: "Stripe", group: "Payments", mode: :api_key, soon: true,
        blurb: "Payments + billing — charge, subscribe, and read revenue from your Stripe account.",
        detail: "Lets agents and apps work against your Stripe account: create charges and subscriptions, read customers + revenue, and wire checkout into a workbook. Connect with a restricted API key.",
        field: "Secret key", hint: "sk_live_…"},
      %{id: "polar", name: "Polar", group: "Payments", mode: :api_key, soon: true,
        blurb: "Merchant-of-record billing for software — products, checkouts, subscriptions.",
        detail: "Polar handles merchant-of-record billing (it takes on the sales-tax/VAT burden): define products, run checkouts, and manage subscriptions for a workbook. Connect with an organization access token.",
        field: "Access token", hint: "polar_oat_…"}
    ]
  end

  def spec(provider), do: Enum.find(catalog(), &(&1.id == provider))

  # ── connection profiles — the transparency + policy layer behind the "connection details" page ───
  #
  # A profile normalises ANY backing (vendored CLI, OAuth API, API key, self-authored toolkit) to one
  # shape so the details page renders identically:
  #
  #   %{provider, name, backing, cli, summary, data, grants, consumed_by, command_grants}
  #
  # A `grant` is the unit of permission a tenant can toggle on the details page; its `key` is what
  # `Nexus.ConnectionPolicy` stores in the per-connection allow-list and enforces at the seam:
  #
  #   * backing "cli"     → key is a command group of the vendored CLI (`gws gmail` → "gmail"),
  #                         enforced at the host_exec seam via `ConnectionPolicy.argv_grant/2`.
  #   * backing "oauth"   → key is the OAuth scope string, enforced at token-handout (`fresh_token/3`).
  #   * backing "api_key" → a single "use" grant (the key is unscoped at the provider; pausing it
  #                         withholds the token entirely).
  #   * backing "self"    → no external grant; the surface is the toolkit's own skills.
  #
  # CLI-first: a provider that ships a real CLI is policed by its command tree (the richer, naturally
  # enforceable surface). OpenAPI/scope strings are the fallback for API-only providers.

  # Per-OAuth-scope documentation: plain-English label + what it actually grants + read/write/admin.
  # Transcribed facts from each provider's scope docs (same provenance as the catalog endpoints).
  @scope_docs %{
    # GitHub
    "repo" => %{label: "Repositories", summary: "Read & write code, issues, pull requests and releases across public and private repos.", access: "write", group: "Code"},
    "read:org" => %{label: "Org & teams", summary: "Read organization membership and team structure.", access: "read", group: "Org"},
    "workflow" => %{label: "Actions workflows", summary: "Read & update GitHub Actions workflow files.", access: "write", group: "CI"},
    # Slack
    "channels:read" => %{label: "Channels", summary: "List public channels and read their metadata.", access: "read", group: "Messaging"},
    "chat:write" => %{label: "Post messages", summary: "Send messages as the connected app.", access: "write", group: "Messaging"},
    "users:read" => %{label: "Users", summary: "Read the member directory.", access: "read", group: "Directory"},
    # Linear
    "read" => %{label: "Read", summary: "Read issues, cycles and projects.", access: "read", group: "Issues"},
    "write" => %{label: "Write", summary: "Create and update issues (behind an approval gate).", access: "write", group: "Issues"},
    # Google (3-legged personal)
    "openid" => %{label: "Identity", summary: "Verify who you are.", access: "read", group: "Identity"},
    "email" => %{label: "Email address", summary: "Read your account email address.", access: "read", group: "Identity"},
    "profile" => %{label: "Profile", summary: "Read your basic profile.", access: "read", group: "Identity"},
    "https://www.googleapis.com/auth/gmail.modify" => %{label: "Mail", summary: "Read, send and organise your mail.", access: "write", group: "Gmail"},
    "https://www.googleapis.com/auth/drive.file" => %{label: "Drive (its files)", summary: "Read & write only the Drive files it creates.", access: "write", group: "Drive"},
    "https://www.googleapis.com/auth/cloud-platform" => %{label: "Google Cloud", summary: "Operate Google Cloud resources.", access: "admin", group: "Cloud"},
    "https://www.googleapis.com/auth/admin.directory.user" => %{label: "Directory users", summary: "Create & manage Workspace users.", access: "admin", group: "Admin"}
  }

  # CLI-backed providers — the command tree IS the permission surface. Each entry: command group →
  # grant (key/label/summary/access/group). `gws` is the Google Workspace CLI (github.com/googleworkspace/cli):
  # one Rust CLI, command surface generated from Google's Discovery service, "built for humans and AI agents".
  @cli_profiles %{
    "google" => %{
      cli: "gws",
      summary: "Run the Google Workspace CLI (gws) against your domain — Drive, Gmail, Calendar, Sheets, Docs, Chat and Admin under one connection.",
      data: ["Drive files", "Email & threads", "Calendar events", "Sheets & Docs", "Directory: users & groups"],
      groups: [
        %{key: "drive", label: "Drive", summary: "List, read, upload and share Drive files.", access: "write", group: "Files"},
        %{key: "gmail", label: "Gmail", summary: "Read, send and organise mail.", access: "write", group: "Mail"},
        %{key: "calendar", label: "Calendar", summary: "Read and manage events.", access: "write", group: "Calendar"},
        %{key: "sheets", label: "Sheets", summary: "Read and write spreadsheets.", access: "write", group: "Docs"},
        %{key: "docs", label: "Docs", summary: "Read and write documents.", access: "write", group: "Docs"},
        %{key: "chat", label: "Chat", summary: "Read and post in Chat spaces.", access: "write", group: "Messaging"},
        %{key: "admin", label: "Admin", summary: "Manage Workspace users, groups and domains.", access: "admin", group: "Admin"}
      ]
    }
  }

  # Which built-in toolkits draw on a given connection (provider id → toolkit ids). Hand-mapped; this
  # is what the details page lists under "Used by" so a tenant sees the blast radius of a restriction.
  @connection_consumers %{
    "github" => ["git"],
    "google" => ["google-workspace"],
    "gmail" => [],
    "slack" => [],
    "cloudflare" => ["cloudflare", "wrangler"],
    "linear" => ["linear"],
    "fal" => ["image-generation", "video-generation", "speech"],
    "openrouter" => []
  }

  @doc """
  The normalised connection profile for a catalog provider id. `nil` if unknown.

  Drives both the documentation (what's connected, what's reachable) and the policy checklist
  (each `grant.key` is what `Nexus.ConnectionPolicy` gates). CLI-backed first, then OAuth scopes,
  then a single API-key grant.
  """
  def connection_profile(provider) do
    case spec(provider) do
      nil -> nil
      s -> build_profile(s)
    end
  end

  defp build_profile(%{id: id, name: name} = s) do
    base = %{provider: id, name: name, consumed_by: Map.get(@connection_consumers, id, [])}

    cond do
      cli = @cli_profiles[id] ->
        grants = Enum.map(cli.groups, &Map.put(&1, :group, &1.group))

        Map.merge(base, %{
          backing: "cli",
          cli: cli.cli,
          summary: cli.summary,
          data: cli.data,
          grants: grants,
          command_grants: Map.new(cli.groups, &{&1.key, &1.key}),
          # BYO-auth: how the customer connects their OWN credential (no vendored client id).
          setup_url: s[:setup_url],
          cred_kinds: Map.get(s, :cred_kinds, [])
        })

      s.mode == :oauth2 ->
        scopes = String.split(to_string(s[:scopes] || ""), [" ", ","], trim: true)
        grants = Enum.map(scopes, &scope_grant/1)

        Map.merge(base, %{
          backing: "oauth",
          cli: nil,
          summary: s[:detail] || s.blurb,
          data: Enum.map(grants, & &1.label) |> Enum.uniq(),
          grants: grants,
          command_grants: %{}
        })

      true ->
        # API-key: the pasted token is unscoped at the provider. One "use" grant — pausing it
        # withholds the token from every toolkit; a true scope split would need a scoped token.
        Map.merge(base, %{
          backing: "api_key",
          cli: nil,
          summary: s[:detail] || s.blurb,
          data: [s.blurb],
          grants: [%{key: "use", label: "Use this key", summary: "Allow toolkits to call #{name} with this key. Turn off to pause it without disconnecting.", access: "write", group: "Access"}],
          command_grants: %{}
        })
    end
  end

  defp scope_grant(scope) do
    case @scope_docs[scope] do
      nil -> %{key: scope, label: scope_tail(scope), summary: "Provider scope: #{scope}.", access: "read", group: "Other"}
      doc -> Map.put(doc, :key, scope)
    end
  end

  defp scope_tail(scope), do: scope |> String.replace("https://www.googleapis.com/auth/", "") |> String.split("/") |> List.last()

  # All grant keys a profile exposes — the valid set `ConnectionPolicy.set/4` intersects against.
  defp profile_keys(%{grants: grants}), do: Enum.map(grants, & &1.key)
  defp profile_keys(_), do: []

  # ── the built-in TOOLKIT catalog — capabilities that need no external account (the other KIND). Only
  # genuinely-shipping ones; sandbox/git/ffmpeg are core nexus infrastructure, not add-ons, so they're
  # not listed here. These are default-added (enabled) and can be disabled per tenant.
  # >>> GENERATED toolkit registry — DO NOT EDIT BY HAND.
  # Source of truth: toolkits/*/manifest.html with a `data-dashboard="<blurb>"` attr.
  # Regenerate: python3 toolkits/gen_dashboard_registry.py
  def standalone_catalog do
    [
      %{id: "presentation", name: "Presentation", status: "ready", default: true,
        cap_kind: "skill", requires: [], recommends: nil,
        blurb: "Build reveal.js decks straight from your content.",
        detail: "Ask for a presentation, get reveal.js — slide nav, fullscreen, per-slide URLs. The capability is \"presentation\"; the impl is reveal.js, swappable."},
      %{id: "video", name: "Video Editing", status: "ready", default: true,
        cap_kind: "skill", requires: ["vision"], recommends: nil,
        blurb: "Author and render video inside the nexus on our own HTML-to-video engine — no external tools or accounts.",
        detail: "Ask for a video, get a finished clip — you author a scene as HTML on a fixed canvas, and our in-nexus engine renders it to video. No external tooling or accounts; the rendering engine is ours, and swappable."},
      %{id: "research", name: "Research", status: "ready", default: true,
        cap_kind: "agent-led", requires: [], recommends: nil,
        blurb: "Multi-source web research → organized, cited findings.",
        detail: "Ask a question, get a structured cited brief — the Deep Researcher fans out searches, reads sources, and synthesizes findings into an organized answer, not a doc dump. Ships its own agent, no account."},
      %{id: "toolkit-forge", name: "Toolkit Forge", status: "ready", default: true,
        cap_kind: "skill", requires: [], recommends: nil,
        blurb: "Forge a new toolkit from an npm package, GitHub URL, or a need.",
        detail: "Forge a new toolkit from a target — npm name / GitHub URL / semantic need — by research → study real surface → author deep skills + manifest to the toolkits/AUTHORING.md standard."},
      %{id: "image-generation", name: "Image Generation", status: "ready", default: true,
        cap_kind: "agent-led", requires: [], recommends: nil,
        blurb: "Generate images from a prompt — routes to the right image model.",
        detail: "Ask for an image, get one — this capability routes a text prompt to a text-to-image model on the gateway (Flux, SDXL, Ideogram, …), chosen by quality + cost, and returns the asset. The model roster is the live text-to-image set; per-model skill context improves over time."},
      %{id: "speech", name: "Speech", status: "ready", default: true,
        cap_kind: "agent-led", requires: [], recommends: nil,
        blurb: "Turn text into spoken audio — routes to the right voice model.",
        detail: "Ask for narration / a voiceover / spoken audio, get a clip — this capability routes text to a text-to-speech model on the gateway (Aura, MeloTTS, …), chosen by voice + cost, and returns the audio. Live roster, improvable skill context."},
      %{id: "video-generation", name: "Video Generation", status: "ready", default: true,
        cap_kind: "agent-led", requires: [], recommends: nil,
        blurb: "Generate video from a prompt or image — routes to the right video model.",
        detail: "Ask for a generated clip, get one — this capability routes a prompt (or a still) to a text-to-video / image-to-video model on the gateway (Runway, Kling, Krea, …), chosen by length + cost, and returns the clip. Distinct from the HyperFrames `video` toolkit, which authors deterministic motion graphics; this one is generative AI video. Live roster, improvable skill context."}
    ]
  end
  # <<< GENERATED

  # Built-in toolkits are default-added; a tenant can disable one (stored as a :toolkit record with
  # enabled:false). `enabled?/2` resolves the effective state.
  defp toolkit_enabled?(tenant, id) do
    case CP.get(tenant, :toolkit, id) do
      {:ok, rec} -> rec[:enabled] != false
      _ -> true
    end
  end

  @doc "Toggle a built-in toolkit on/off for this tenant. `{200, %{id, enabled}}`."
  def toggle_toolkit(req) do
    id = req.params["id"]

    if Enum.any?(standalone_catalog(), &(&1.id == id)) do
      now = !toolkit_enabled?(req.tenant, id)
      CP.put(req.tenant, :toolkit, id, %{enabled: now})
      {200, %{id: id, enabled: now}}
    else
      {404, %{error: "no such toolkit"}}
    end
  end

  # ── unified toolkit library — the one feed behind the dashboard's Toolkits page. A toolkit comes in
  # two KINDS: a PROVIDER toolkit wraps an external service (the integrations above — connect an account),
  # a STANDALONE toolkit needs none. `status` is "ready" | "soon" so the UI can mark unfinished work.
  def toolkits(req) do
    conns = CP.list(req.tenant, :integration)

    providers =
      for p <- catalog() do
        accounts =
          conns
          |> Enum.filter(&(&1[:provider] == p.id))
          |> Enum.map(&%{id: &1[:id], label: &1[:label], status: &1[:status] || "connected"})

        ready = configured?(p) and not Map.get(p, :soon, false)

        p
        |> Map.take([:id, :name, :group, :blurb, :detail, :field, :hint])
        |> Map.merge(%{
          kind: "provider",
          mode: Atom.to_string(p.mode),
          configured: configured?(p),
          status: if(ready, do: "ready", else: "soon"),
          accounts: accounts
        })
      end

    standalone =
      Enum.map(standalone_catalog(), fn t ->
        t |> Map.put(:kind, "standalone") |> Map.put(:enabled, toolkit_enabled?(req.tenant, t.id))
      end)

    %{toolkits: providers ++ standalone}
  end

  # ── connection details — the transparency page. Keyed by CATALOG id (provider or standalone). ────
  # For a provider: the normalised profile (what's reachable, grants with plain-English meaning,
  # which toolkits use it) PLUS every connected account with its current allow-list, so the page can
  # render a per-account permission checklist. For a standalone (self-authored) toolkit: its own
  # capability surface — no external grants, no scopes to police.
  def toolkit_detail(req) do
    id = req.params["id"]

    cond do
      not is_nil(connection_profile(id)) ->
        profile = connection_profile(id)
        keys = profile_keys(profile)

        accounts =
          req.tenant
          |> CP.list(:integration)
          |> Enum.filter(&(&1[:provider] == id))
          |> Enum.map(fn rec ->
            allowed =
              case Nexus.ConnectionPolicy.allowed_list(rec) do
                :all -> keys
                list -> list
              end

            %{id: rec[:id], label: rec[:label], restricted: rec[:allowed] != nil, allowed: allowed}
          end)

        {200, Map.merge(profile, %{kind: "provider", accounts: accounts})}

      t = Enum.find(standalone_catalog(), &(&1.id == id)) ->
        {200,
         %{
           kind: "standalone",
           provider: t.id,
           name: t.name,
           backing: "self",
           cli: nil,
           summary: t.detail || t.blurb,
           data: [],
           grants: [],
           consumed_by: [],
           requires: Map.get(t, :requires, []),
           enabled: toolkit_enabled?(req.tenant, t.id)
         }}

      true ->
        {404, %{error: "no such toolkit"}}
    end
  end

  @doc """
  Set a connection's permission allow-list (the details-page checklist). Body: `%{allowed: [key]}`.
  Keys are intersected against the connection's real grant surface, so a stale/forged key cannot
  widen access. The runtime `Nexus.ConnectionPolicy` enforces the result at the token/exec seam.
  """
  def set_scopes(req) do
    id = req.params["id"]
    allowed = (req.body || %{})["allowed"]

    with {:ok, rec} <- CP.get(req.tenant, :integration, id),
         profile when not is_nil(profile) <- connection_profile(rec[:provider]),
         true <- is_list(allowed) do
      keys = Enum.map(allowed, &to_string/1)

      case Nexus.ConnectionPolicy.set(req.tenant, id, keys, valid_keys: profile_keys(profile)) do
        {:ok, eff} -> {200, %{id: id, allowed: eff}}
        {:error, reason} -> {400, %{error: to_string(reason)}}
      end
    else
      {:error, :not_found} -> {404, %{error: "no such connection"}}
      nil -> {400, %{error: "connection has no policy profile"}}
      false -> {400, %{error: "allowed must be a list of grant keys"}}
      _ -> {400, %{error: "could not set scopes"}}
    end
  end

  # ── list — the marketplace state: every provider + the accounts wired up under it ──────────────
  def list_integrations(req) do
    conns = CP.list(req.tenant, :integration)

    providers =
      for p <- catalog() do
        accounts =
          conns
          |> Enum.filter(&(&1[:provider] == p.id))
          |> Enum.map(&%{id: &1[:id], label: &1[:label], status: &1[:status] || "connected"})

        p
        |> Map.take([:id, :name, :group, :blurb, :field, :hint])
        |> Map.put(:mode, Atom.to_string(p.mode))
        |> Map.put(:configured, configured?(p))
        |> Map.put(:accounts, accounts)
      end

    %{providers: providers}
  end

  # API-key providers are always ready (you paste). OAuth providers need this nexus's app credentials.
  defp configured?(%{mode: :api_key}), do: true
  # CLI / bring-your-own-credentials providers are ALWAYS ready — there is nothing for the nexus to
  # configure. The customer brings their own credential (e.g. their own Google Cloud project for gws);
  # we vendor no client id and require no platform secret. This is the whole point of the BYO model.
  defp configured?(%{mode: :cli}), do: true
  defp configured?(%{mode: :oauth2, id: id}), do: Nexus.Secrets.has?(client_id_secret(id))

  # ── API-key connect — seal the pasted token, store the connection ──────────────────────────────
  def connect_api_key(req) do
    b = req.body || %{}
    provider = b["provider"]
    label = String.trim(to_string(b["label"] || ""))
    credential = to_string(b["credential"] || "")
    s = spec(provider)

    cond do
      is_nil(s) or s.mode != :api_key -> {400, %{error: "not an API-key provider"}}
      label == "" -> {400, %{error: "name this account"}}
      credential == "" -> {400, %{error: "a credential is required"}}
      true ->
        id = new_id()

        case seal(req.tenant, id, "TOKEN", credential) do
          {:ok, sid} ->
            store_connection(req.tenant, id, provider, label, %{token_secret_id: sid})
            {201, %{id: id, provider: provider, label: label, status: "connected"}}

          {:error, reason} ->
            {seal_status(reason), %{error: to_string(reason)}}
        end
    end
  end

  # ── CLI (bring-your-own) connect — seal the customer's own credential, store which env var feeds it ──
  # No vendored client id, no OAuth dance: the customer set up auth on their side (their own Google Cloud
  # project, via `gws auth setup`) and brings the result — an access token or a credentials JSON. We seal
  # it and record the `cred_kind` so `connection_env/2` knows which env var a sandboxed CLI run needs.
  def connect_cli(req) do
    b = req.body || %{}
    provider = b["provider"]
    label = String.trim(to_string(b["label"] || ""))
    credential = to_string(b["credential"] || "")
    cred_kind = to_string(b["cred_kind"] || "")
    s = spec(provider)
    kind = s && Enum.find(Map.get(s, :cred_kinds, []), &(&1.id == cred_kind))

    cond do
      is_nil(s) or s.mode != :cli -> {400, %{error: "not a CLI provider"}}
      label == "" -> {400, %{error: "name this connection"}}
      credential == "" -> {400, %{error: "a credential is required"}}
      is_nil(kind) -> {400, %{error: "unknown credential kind"}}
      true ->
        id = new_id()

        case seal(req.tenant, id, "CRED", credential) do
          {:ok, sid} ->
            # Denormalise the profile bits the RUNTIME exec seam needs (cli binary, command→grant map,
            # the toolkits that consume this connection) onto the record, so `Nexus.CliConnections` can
            # build the run opts generically — without reaching back into this app's profile catalog.
            profile = connection_profile(provider)

            store_connection(req.tenant, id, provider, label, %{
              token_secret_id: sid,
              cred_kind: cred_kind,
              env_var: kind.env,
              cli: profile[:cli],
              command_grants: Map.get(profile, :command_grants, %{}),
              consumer_toolkits: Map.get(@connection_consumers, provider, [])
            })

            {201, %{id: id, provider: provider, label: label, status: "connected"}}

          {:error, reason} ->
            {seal_status(reason), %{error: to_string(reason)}}
        end
    end
  end

  @doc """
  The credential-injection contract — what a sandboxed CLI run needs to authenticate as this
  connection. Returns `{:ok, %{env: %{name => value}, files: [%{path, content, env}]}}`:

    * `env`   — environment variables to set in the guest (e.g. `GOOGLE_WORKSPACE_CLI_TOKEN`).
    * `files` — credentials that must be a file on disk: write `content` to `path` inside the run's
      `/work` view, then set the named env var to that path (`GOOGLE_WORKSPACE_CLI_CREDENTIALS_FILE`).

  The runtime's exec seam consumes this when it launches the CLI; this function is the single place
  that turns a sealed connection into runnable credentials, so no toolkit ever sees the raw secret
  except as its own process env. Honours the per-connection scope policy is the caller's job (the
  CLI seam gates command groups via `Nexus.ConnectionPolicy.argv_grant/2`).
  """
  @doc """
  Turn a CLI-backed connection into the run options a sandboxed shell needs — credentials injected as
  env, scope enforced as an exec policy — ready to splat into `Nexus.Shell.run/3`. Thin wrapper over the
  generic runtime resolver `Nexus.CliConnections`, which builds the opts from the fields `connect_cli`
  denormalised onto the record (so the open-standard runtime carries no provider catalog). The runtime
  binds the active connection by `caps` automatically; this app-facing form resolves a specific id (used
  by tests + any explicit invocation). Returns `{:ok, [env:, exec_policy:, files:]}` or `{:error, …}`.
  """
  def run_context(tenant, conn_id) do
    case Nexus.CliConnections.opts_for_id(tenant, conn_id) do
      [] -> {:error, :no_run_context}
      opts -> {:ok, opts}
    end
  end

  # ── OAuth: step 1 — mint state, hand back the provider authorize URL for the popup ─────────────
  def authorize(req) do
    provider = req.query["provider"]
    label = String.trim(to_string(req.query["label"] || ""))
    origin = to_string(req.query["origin"] || "")
    s = spec(provider)

    cond do
      is_nil(s) or s.mode != :oauth2 -> {400, %{error: "not an OAuth provider"}}
      not configured?(s) -> {400, %{error: "#{s.name} OAuth app not configured (set #{client_id_secret(provider)} / #{client_secret_secret(provider)})"}}
      origin == "" -> {400, %{error: "origin required"}}
      true ->
        state = new_state()
        redirect_uri = origin <> "/cloud/oauth/callback"
        # Park the handshake so the callback can resolve tenant + the exact redirect_uri used.
        CP.put(@oauth_org, :oauth_state, state, %{
          tenant: req.tenant, provider: provider, label: label, redirect_uri: redirect_uri
        })

        params =
          Map.merge(s.auth_params, %{
            "client_id" => Nexus.Secrets.get(client_id_secret(provider)),
            "redirect_uri" => redirect_uri,
            "response_type" => "code",
            "scope" => s.scopes,
            "state" => state
          })

        {200, %{url: s.authorize_url <> "?" <> URI.encode_query(params)}}
    end
  end

  # ── OAuth: step 2 — provider redirects here; exchange the code server-side, seal the tokens ─────
  def callback(req) do
    code = req.query["code"]
    state = req.query["state"]

    with {:ok, pend} <- CP.get(@oauth_org, :oauth_state, state),
         true <- is_binary(code) and code != "",
         s when not is_nil(s) <- spec(pend.provider),
         {:ok, tokens} <- exchange_code(s, code, pend.redirect_uri) do
      CP.delete(@oauth_org, :oauth_state, state)
      persist_oauth(pend.tenant, pend.provider, pend.label, tokens)
      popup_done("Connected — you can close this window.")
    else
      _ ->
        if state, do: CP.delete(@oauth_org, :oauth_state, state)
        popup_done("Connection failed. Close this window and try again.")
    end
  end

  # Seal access + refresh as TWO separate encrypted secrets; expiry (not sensitive) on the record.
  defp persist_oauth(tenant, provider, label, tokens) do
    id = new_id()
    {:ok, asid} = seal(tenant, id, "ACCESS", tokens.access)
    refresh_id = if tokens.refresh, do: with({:ok, rid} <- seal(tenant, id, "REFRESH", tokens.refresh), do: rid)

    store_connection(tenant, id, provider, account_label(provider, label, tokens.access), %{
      access_secret_id: asid,
      refresh_secret_id: refresh_id,
      expires_at: tokens.expires_at
    })
  end

  # The connection's display name. If the popup supplied one, keep it; otherwise derive it from the
  # authorized identity (GitHub login, Google email) so connecting is just Connect → OAuth, no naming.
  defp account_label(provider, label, access) do
    case String.trim(to_string(label)) do
      "" -> derive_label(provider, access) || String.capitalize(provider)
      l -> l
    end
  end

  defp derive_label("github", token),
    do: identity_field("https://api.github.com/user", token, "login")

  defp derive_label("gmail", token),
    do: identity_field("https://www.googleapis.com/oauth2/v2/userinfo", token, "email")

  defp derive_label(_provider, _token), do: nil

  # GET a provider identity endpoint with the fresh access token and pull one field for the label.
  defp identity_field(url, token, key) do
    :inets.start()
    :ssl.start()

    headers = [
      {~c"authorization", ~c"Bearer " ++ to_charlist(token)},
      {~c"accept", ~c"application/json"},
      {~c"user-agent", ~c"workbooks-nexus"}
    ]

    case :httpc.request(:get, {to_charlist(url), headers}, [timeout: 10_000, ssl: [verify: :verify_none]], body_format: :binary) do
      {:ok, {{_, code, _}, _h, body}} when code in 200..299 ->
        case Jason.decode(body) do
          {:ok, m} when is_map(m) -> m[key]
          _ -> nil
        end

      _ ->
        nil
    end
  rescue
    _ -> nil
  end

  # ── refresh — the seam toolkits call. Returns a live access token, refreshing if expired. ───────
  # `{:ok, token} | {:error, reason}`. Server-side only; never exposed as a plaintext-returning route.
  def fresh_token(tenant, id) do
    with {:ok, rec} <- CP.get(tenant, :integration, id) do
      cond do
        rec[:token_secret_id] -> Env.reveal(tenant, rec.token_secret_id)
        expired?(rec) and rec[:refresh_secret_id] -> refresh(tenant, rec)
        rec[:access_secret_id] -> Env.reveal(tenant, rec.access_secret_id)
        true -> {:error, :no_token}
      end
    end
  end

  @doc """
  Grant-gated variant — the seam a toolkit calls when it knows which grant it's exercising
  (an OAuth scope / endpoint group, or an API-key "use"). Returns `{:error, :scope_blocked}` if
  the tenant has restricted this connection's allow-list to exclude `grant`, before any token is
  revealed. CLI-backed connections enforce at the host_exec seam instead, not here.
  """
  def fresh_token(tenant, id, grant) when is_binary(grant) do
    if Nexus.ConnectionPolicy.allowed?(tenant, id, grant) do
      fresh_token(tenant, id)
    else
      {:error, :scope_blocked}
    end
  end

  defp expired?(%{expires_at: at}) when is_integer(at), do: System.os_time(:second) >= at - 60
  defp expired?(_), do: false

  defp refresh(tenant, rec) do
    s = spec(rec.provider)

    with {:ok, refresh_tok} <- Env.reveal(tenant, rec.refresh_secret_id),
         {:ok, tokens} <- refresh_code(s, refresh_tok) do
      Env.update(tenant, rec.access_secret_id, %{value: tokens.access})
      new_refresh_id = rec.refresh_secret_id

      if tokens.refresh,
        do: Env.update(tenant, rec.refresh_secret_id, %{value: tokens.refresh})

      CP.update(tenant, :integration, rec.id, %{expires_at: tokens.expires_at, refresh_secret_id: new_refresh_id})
      {:ok, tokens.access}
    end
  end

  # ── disconnect — drop the sealed secrets, then the record ──────────────────────────────────────
  def remove_integration(req) do
    id = req.params["id"]

    case CP.get(req.tenant, :integration, id) do
      {:ok, rec} ->
        for k <- [:token_secret_id, :access_secret_id, :refresh_secret_id],
            sid = rec[k],
            is_binary(sid),
            do: Env.delete(req.tenant, sid)

        CP.delete(req.tenant, :integration, id)
        %{ok: true, id: id}

      {:error, :not_found} ->
        {404, %{error: "no such connection"}}
    end
  end

  # ── token endpoints (HTTP) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  defp exchange_code(s, code, redirect_uri) do
    form = %{
      "grant_type" => "authorization_code",
      "code" => code,
      "redirect_uri" => redirect_uri,
      "client_id" => Nexus.Secrets.get(client_id_secret(s.id)),
      "client_secret" => Nexus.Secrets.get(client_secret_secret(s.id))
    }

    post_token(s.token_url, form)
  end

  defp refresh_code(s, refresh_tok) do
    form = %{
      "grant_type" => "refresh_token",
      "refresh_token" => refresh_tok,
      "client_id" => Nexus.Secrets.get(client_id_secret(s.id)),
      "client_secret" => Nexus.Secrets.get(client_secret_secret(s.id))
    }

    post_token(s.token_url, form)
  end

  # POST the form to the provider's token endpoint and normalise the bundle. `Accept: application/json`
  # makes GitHub answer JSON (it defaults to form-encoded); everyone else already does.
  defp post_token(url, form) do
    :inets.start()
    :ssl.start()
    body = URI.encode_query(form)

    headers = [
      {~c"accept", ~c"application/json"},
      {~c"user-agent", ~c"workbooks-nexus"}
    ]

    req = {to_charlist(url), headers, ~c"application/x-www-form-urlencoded", body}

    case :httpc.request(:post, req, [timeout: 15_000, ssl: [verify: :verify_none]], body_format: :binary) do
      {:ok, {{_, code, _}, _h, resp}} when code in 200..299 ->
        case Jason.decode(resp) do
          {:ok, %{"access_token" => access} = m} ->
            exp = m["expires_in"]
            {:ok, %{
              access: access,
              refresh: m["refresh_token"],
              expires_at: if(is_integer(exp), do: System.os_time(:second) + exp, else: nil)
            }}

          _ ->
            {:error, :bad_token_response}
        end

      {:ok, {{_, code, _}, _h, _resp}} ->
        {:error, {:token_http, code}}

      {:error, e} ->
        {:error, inspect(e)}
    end
  end

  # ── Google Workspace via domain-wide delegation ────────────────────────────────────────────────
  # The cheapest/fastest broad-access path: ONE service account (ours), the customer's super-admin
  # authorizes its client id + these scopes once in their Admin console, and the agent then impersonates
  # any user in the domain (create mailboxes, manage aliases, drive Admin SDK/Gmail). No per-user OAuth,
  # no Marketplace listing required. Tokens are minted on demand by `Nexus.Google` — nothing to seal.
  # Scope minimisation (cheapest = no CASA): drive.file not full drive, gmail.modify/settings not
  # mail.google.com/. admin.directory.* + cloud-platform are "sensitive" (verification, no CASA).
  @dwd_scopes [
    "openid",
    "email",
    "https://www.googleapis.com/auth/admin.directory.user",
    "https://www.googleapis.com/auth/admin.directory.user.alias",
    "https://www.googleapis.com/auth/admin.directory.domain",
    "https://www.googleapis.com/auth/admin.directory.group",
    "https://www.googleapis.com/auth/gmail.settings.basic",
    "https://www.googleapis.com/auth/gmail.settings.sharing",
    "https://www.googleapis.com/auth/gmail.modify",
    "https://www.googleapis.com/auth/drive.file",
    "https://www.googleapis.com/auth/cloud-platform"
  ]

  @doc "What the dashboard shows so a customer's admin can wire delegation: the SA client id + scopes."
  def google_delegation(_req) do
    %{
      configured: Nexus.Google.configured?(),
      client_id: Nexus.Google.client_id(),
      scopes: @dwd_scopes,
      scopes_csv: Enum.join(@dwd_scopes, ","),
      admin_console_url: "https://admin.google.com/ac/owl/domainwidedelegation"
    }
  end

  # Record a delegation connection: the only per-tenant facts are the domain + the admin user we
  # impersonate for Admin SDK calls. No tokens stored — `google_token/3` mints them on demand.
  def connect_google_dwd(req) do
    b = req.body || %{}
    domain = String.trim(to_string(b["domain"] || "")) |> String.downcase()
    admin = String.trim(to_string(b["admin_email"] || "")) |> String.downcase()

    cond do
      not Nexus.Google.configured?() -> {503, %{error: "delegation service account not configured on this nexus"}}
      domain == "" -> {400, %{error: "your Workspace domain is required"}}
      not String.contains?(admin, "@") -> {400, %{error: "a super-admin email to impersonate is required"}}
      true ->
        id = new_id()
        store_connection(req.tenant, id, "google", domain, %{mode: "dwd", admin_email: admin})
        {201, %{id: id, provider: "google", label: domain, status: "connected", mode: "dwd"}}
    end
  end

  # Create a dedicated agent mailbox on the connected domain. Impersonates the stored super-admin for the
  # Admin SDK call. WARNS the caller this consumes a paid Workspace seat (billed to the customer).
  def google_create_mailbox(req) do
    b = req.body || %{}
    email = String.trim(to_string(b["email"] || ""))

    cond do
      email == "" -> {400, %{error: "an email for the mailbox is required"}}
      true ->
        with {:ok, token} <- google_token(req.tenant, req.params["id"], "https://www.googleapis.com/auth/admin.directory.user") do
          attrs = %{primary_email: email, given_name: b["given_name"] || "Workbooks", family_name: b["family_name"] || "Agent"}

          case Nexus.Google.Admin.create_user(token, attrs) do
            {:ok, user} -> {201, %{ok: true, user: user, note: "this mailbox consumes a paid Workspace seat"}}
            {:error, reason} -> {502, %{error: inspect(reason)}}
          end
        else
          {:error, reason} -> {502, %{error: inspect(reason)}}
        end
    end
  end

  # Add a free proxy alias to an existing user on the connected domain.
  def google_add_alias(req) do
    b = req.body || %{}
    user = String.trim(to_string(b["user"] || ""))
    alias_email = String.trim(to_string(b["alias"] || ""))

    cond do
      user == "" or alias_email == "" -> {400, %{error: "user + alias are required"}}
      true ->
        with {:ok, token} <- google_token(req.tenant, req.params["id"], "https://www.googleapis.com/auth/admin.directory.user.alias") do
          case Nexus.Google.Admin.add_alias(token, user, alias_email) do
            {:ok, _} -> {201, %{ok: true, user: user, alias: alias_email}}
            {:error, reason} -> {502, %{error: inspect(reason)}}
          end
        else
          {:error, reason} -> {502, %{error: inspect(reason)}}
        end
    end
  end

  # Grant a mailbox the ability to send as another address (impersonates that mailbox, not the admin).
  def google_add_sendas(req) do
    b = req.body || %{}
    user = String.trim(to_string(b["user"] || ""))
    send_as = String.trim(to_string(b["send_as"] || ""))

    cond do
      user == "" or send_as == "" -> {400, %{error: "user + send_as are required"}}
      true ->
        with {:ok, token} <- google_token(req.tenant, req.params["id"], "https://www.googleapis.com/auth/gmail.settings.sharing", user) do
          case Nexus.Google.Admin.add_send_as(token, user, send_as) do
            {:ok, _} -> {201, %{ok: true, user: user, send_as: send_as}}
            {:error, reason} -> {502, %{error: inspect(reason)}}
          end
        else
          {:error, reason} -> {502, %{error: inspect(reason)}}
        end
    end
  end

  @doc """
  Server-side seam toolkits call to act on a Google connection. Mints an impersonated access token for
  `scopes`, acting as `sub` (defaults to the stored admin for domain ops). `{:ok, token} | {:error, _}`.
  """
  def google_token(tenant, conn_id, scopes, sub \\ nil) do
    with {:ok, rec} <- CP.get(tenant, :integration, conn_id),
         "dwd" <- rec[:mode] || "dwd",
         impersonate when is_binary(impersonate) <- sub || rec[:admin_email] do
      case Nexus.Google.access_token(impersonate, scopes) do
        {:ok, %{access: token}} -> {:ok, token}
        {:skip, reason} -> {:error, reason}
        err -> err
      end
    else
      {:error, _} = err -> err
      _ -> {:error, :not_a_delegation_connection}
    end
  end

  # ── shared helpers ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  defp store_connection(tenant, id, provider, label, extra) do
    attrs = Map.merge(%{provider: provider, label: label, status: "connected"}, extra)
    {:ok, rec} = CP.put(tenant, :integration, id, attrs)
    rec
  end

  # Seal one value into the encrypted Env store under a per-connection name; returns its secret id.
  defp seal(tenant, conn_id, suffix, value) do
    name = "#{String.upcase(conn_id)}__#{suffix}" |> String.replace(~r/[^A-Z0-9_]/, "_")

    case Env.create(tenant, %{name: name, value: value, scope: "user"}) do
      {:ok, env} -> {:ok, env[:id]}
      err -> err
    end
  end

  defp seal_status(:no_master_key), do: 503
  defp seal_status(_), do: 400

  # The deploy-injected secret name holding this provider's OAuth client id / secret. We prefer the
  # dedicated `<PROVIDER>_OAUTH_*` name, but fall back to any provider-specific alias so an operator who
  # already injected credentials for a sibling feature need not register a second app. GitHub is the
  # case in point: a GitHub *App* (for repo mirroring) already ships `GITHUB_APP_CLIENT_ID/_SECRET`, and
  # its user-to-server OAuth uses the very same authorize/token endpoints — so the marketplace "Connect"
  # button reuses those creds with no extra registration. (App OAuth ignores the `scope` param and grants
  # the App's installed permissions instead, which is exactly what we want here.)
  # "gmail" is the personal (3-legged) Google connect — it shares the GOOGLE_OAUTH_* web-client creds.
  @oauth_id_aliases %{
    "github" => ["GITHUB_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID", "GITHUB_APP_CLIENT_ID"],
    "gmail" => ["GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID"]
  }
  @oauth_secret_aliases %{
    "github" => ["GITHUB_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET", "GITHUB_APP_CLIENT_SECRET"],
    "gmail" => ["GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET"]
  }

  defp client_id_secret(provider),
    do: resolve_secret_name(provider, @oauth_id_aliases, "#{String.upcase(provider)}_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID")

  defp client_secret_secret(provider),
    do: resolve_secret_name(provider, @oauth_secret_aliases, "#{String.upcase(provider)}_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET")

  # First candidate name that's actually present wins; otherwise the canonical name (so error messages and
  # `configured?/1` report the name an operator is expected to set).
  defp resolve_secret_name(provider, aliases, default) do
    case aliases[provider] do
      names when is_list(names) -> Enum.find(names, default, &Nexus.Secrets.has?/1)
      _ -> default
    end
  end

  defp new_id, do: "intg_" <> rand()
  defp new_state, do: "st_" <> rand()
  defp rand, do: :crypto.strong_rand_bytes(16) |> Base.url_encode64(padding: false)

  defp popup_done(msg) do
    html = """
    <!doctype html><meta charset="utf-8"><title>Workbooks</title>
    <body style="font:15px system-ui;background:#16171a;color:#ecebe5;display:grid;place-items:center;height:100vh;margin:0">
    <p>#{msg}</p>
    <script>try{window.opener&&window.opener.postMessage('wb-integration-connected','*')}catch(e){}; setTimeout(function(){window.close()},800);</script>
    </body>
    """

    {200, "text/html; charset=utf-8", html}
  end
end
